{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:91b1079c3dd6f0c003bb20f34f3783141cbc55926e6e3001ba18eaba3acc169e", "sample_idx": 11, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "\"skill in solving sudoku puzzles may feel like a mental workout, but it carries no physical weight—unlike the looming climate crisis that demands real-world action. While the numbers 1 to 9 fill a grid with precision, the Earth’s atmosphere carries a growing burden of greenhouse gas emissions that no puzzle can solve. The temporary relief offered by isolated acts of conservation is no substitute for systemic change.\n\n\\n\\n\n\nThe twist in this analogy lies in how we prioritize: we spend minutes on a daily grid while ignoring the decades-long accumulation of environmental damage. Each number placed correctly in a box mirrors the small, disciplined choices we must make daily to reduce our carbon footprint. Yet, unlike a puzzle with a single solution, climate change demands collective, sustained effort—no one can solve it alone.\n\n\\n\\n\n\nThe weight of inaction grows heavier with every unchecked emission, and the grid of our planetary systems is beginning to crack. Just as a misplaced digit ruins a sudoku’s integrity, a single policy failure or corporate loophole can unravel progress toward net-zero goals. The Observer’s digital edition may offer a neat solution, but the real puzzle—saving our biosphere—has no easy answers.\n\n\\n\\n\n\nWe must treat the climate emergency not as a sideline challenge, but as the central grid that governs all our choices. The gas we burn, the policies we ignore, the temporary fixes we cling to—all contribute to a larger, unsolvable mess unless we restructure our priorities. The weight of tomorrow rests on the decisions we make today, and unlike a crossword, there’s no ‘buy next week’ version to fix what we break now.\"", "logprobs": [{"token": "\"", "logprob": -2.4437606043647975e-05}, {"token": "skill", "logprob": -0.0016066036